    "result_text": "All tests pass and the build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented the `capacityWarning` flag for the create-owner endpoint. When an owner's city already holds **40\u201349 owners** (approaching the hard limit of 50), the created owner is flagged `capacityWarning: true`; the hard 409 rejection at 50 is unchanged.\n\nChanges, following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions (mirroring the existing `bulkSignupWarning` flag):\n\n1. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `capacityWarning` field (`capacity_warning` column) with getter/setter.\n2. **DB schemas** (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) \u2014 added the `capacity_warning BOOLEAN` column.\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `capacityWarning` boolean to the Owner schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it by name.\n4. **`AssignCapacityWarning.java`** \u2014 new step function that counts owners in the same city (case-insensitive, using the exact same set the hard capacity check counts) and sets the flag `true` when the count is in `[40, 50)`. It runs before save, so the new owner isn't counted \u2014 the warning band ends precisely where the hard rejection begins.\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired `assignCapacityWarning` into the pipeline (after `assignBulkSignupWarning`, before `save`), under the transaction governance like its neighbours.\n\nThe new acceptance test `coreWarnsWhenApproachingCapacity` passes, and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
